WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
A core conflict sits at the heart of
genomic research.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.620
The rigid rules required to protect human
rights constantly collide with the

3
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.940
rapid, boundary-pushing nature of
scientific innovation.

4
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.660
The legal solution is not to halt
scientific progress, but to rigorously

5
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.440
harmonize these competing principles so
they can safely coexist.

6
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.500
This text is Article 13 of the Oviedo
Convention.

7
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:28.680
It serves as the primary regulatory
standard for genetic engineering,

8
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.860
balancing innovation with strict moral
boundaries.

9
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.860
The rule is explicit. Science may modify
the human genome strictly for

10
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.120
preventive, diagnostic, or therapeutic
purposes.

11
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:45.200
It is absolutely forbidden from making
genetic alterations that will be passed

12
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:46.780
down to descendants.

13
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.580
Ultimately, the architecture of research
ethics exists not to restrict the

14
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:57.640
genomic revolution, but to ensure it
permanently serves human dignity while

15
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.620
maximizing its clinical utility for all.

16
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.300
Thank you for joining this module on
genetics and research.
